    "content": {
      "zh-CN": {
        "id": "u1-04-scarcity",
        "locale": "zh-CN",
        "title": "稀缺性",
        "takeaway": "稀缺性不是“某样东西很少”，而是有限资源无法同时满足所有欲望，因此任何经济体都必须选择。",
        "definition": "稀缺性是指可用于生产商品与服务的资源有限，而人们希望满足的需求和欲望相对于这些资源没有止境。",
        "causalChain": [
          "人们希望获得更多商品、服务与生活机会",
          "土地、劳动、资本、时间和企业家才能都有约束",
          "所有目标无法被同时实现",
          "个人、企业与政府必须在可行选项之间选择",
          "每项选择都会放弃次优方案并产生机会成本"
        ],
        "realWorldExample": {
          "title": "淡水在不同用途之间的配置",
          "sourceUrl": "https://sdgs.un.org/goals/goal6",
          "observedAt": "2026-09-29",
          "explanation": "安全饮水、农业、工业和生态系统都需要淡水。即使一个地区拥有水资源，特定时间与地点可安全使用的水仍然有限，因此社会必须决定各用途的优先顺序。"
        },
        "misconceptions": [
          "稀缺不等于绝对短缺；即使货架上有商品，只要资源不足以满足所有可能用途，稀缺仍存在。",
          "金钱不是唯一稀缺资源；时间、技能、自然资源和生产能力同样受限。",
          "贫困会加剧资源约束，但富裕社会也面对稀缺性，因为欲望和资源仍不完全匹配。"
        ],
        "examApplication": [
          "遇到“解释稀缺性”时，应同时写出有限资源、相对无限欲望以及必须选择这三层逻辑。",
          "评价政策时，可追问政策使用了哪些稀缺资源，以及这些资源原本还能用于什么。"
        ],
        "summary": [
          "稀缺性来自资源与欲望之间的相对关系。",
          "稀缺性迫使所有经济主体作出选择。",
          "选择的代价由机会成本表示。"
        ],
        "explanation": "稀缺性是经济学问题的起点。它描述的不是偶发的缺货，而是一种长期存在的约束：我们可以利用的资源、时间与生产能力，不足以完成所有想做的事情。\n\n因此，经济问题总是在比较不同用途。学生把两个小时用于复习经济学，就无法把同样的两个小时完整用于另一门课；政府把一块土地用于建设医院，就不能在同一地点同时建设公园。经济学研究的正是这些选择如何形成、由谁作出，以及它们给不同群体带来什么后果。\n\n判断一个情境是否体现稀缺性时，可以问两个问题：资源是否有多个相互竞争的用途？是否必须放弃至少一个可行选择？若答案都是“是”，就存在经济意义上的稀缺性。"
      },
      "en": {
        "id": "u1-04-scarcity",
        "locale": "en",
        "title": "Scarcity",
        "takeaway": "Scarcity does not simply mean that something is rare; it means finite resources cannot satisfy every want at the same time, so every economy must choose.",
        "definition": "Scarcity is the condition in which the resources available to produce goods and services are limited relative to the needs and wants people would like to satisfy.",
        "causalChain": [
          "People seek more goods, services and opportunities",
          "Land, labour, capital, time and entrepreneurship are constrained",
          "Not every objective can be achieved simultaneously",
          "Households, firms and governments must choose among feasible alternatives",
          "Each choice gives up a next-best alternative and creates an opportunity cost"
        ],
        "realWorldExample": {
          "title": "Allocating freshwater between competing uses",
          "sourceUrl": "https://sdgs.un.org/goals/goal6",
          "observedAt": "2026-09-29",
          "explanation": "Safe drinking water, farming, industry and ecosystems all depend on freshwater. Even where water exists, the usable supply at a particular time and place is limited, so societies must set priorities among competing uses."
        },
        "misconceptions": [
          "Scarcity is not the same as an absolute shortage; it exists whenever resources cannot satisfy every possible use, even if shops have stock.",
          "Money is not the only scarce resource; time, skills, natural resources and productive capacity are constrained too.",
          "Poverty can intensify resource constraints, but affluent societies also face scarcity because resources still do not match every want."
        ],
        "examApplication": [
          "An explanation of scarcity should connect limited resources, relatively unlimited wants and the resulting need to choose.",
          "When evaluating a policy, ask which scarce resources it uses and which alternative uses are therefore given up."
        ],
        "summary": [
          "Scarcity is a relative relationship between resources and wants.",
          "Scarcity forces every economic agent to choose.",
          "Opportunity cost expresses what a choice sacrifices."
        ],
        "explanation": "Scarcity is the starting point of the economic problem. It is not a temporary empty shelf. It is the continuing constraint that available resources, time and productive capacity cannot accomplish everything people would like to do.\n\nEconomic questions therefore compare alternative uses. Two hours spent revising economics cannot also be spent in full on another subject. Land used for a hospital cannot simultaneously provide a park on the same site. Economics studies how such choices are made, who makes them and how their consequences are distributed.\n\nTo identify scarcity in a situation, ask whether a resource has competing uses and whether at least one feasible option must be forgone. If both answers are yes, the situation contains scarcity in the economic sense."
      }
    },
