    "content": {
      "zh-CN": {
        "id": "u1-17-resource-allocation",
        "locale": "zh-CN",
        "title": "资源配置与重新配置",
        "takeaway": "资源配置决定生产要素流向何处；价格、政策或偏好变化会触发重新配置，并可纠正过度或不足配置。",
        "definition": "资源配置是把生产要素分配到不同用途的过程；重新配置是改变既有用途，过度配置和不足配置分别表示相对于社会最优水平投入过多或过少。",
        "causalChain": [
          "不同用途争夺稀缺要素",
          "价格、收益与政策传递相对价值",
          "资源流向预期回报较高的用途",
          "外部成本或收益可能造成过度或不足配置"
        ],
        "realWorldExample": {
          "title": "碳定价推动资源重新配置",
          "sourceUrl": "https://carbonpricingdashboard.worldbank.org/",
          "observedAt": "2026-09-29",
          "explanation": "世界银行碳定价仪表板记录碳税和排放交易制度。排放成本进入决策后，高碳生产相对回报下降，资本和创新可能转向低碳用途。"
        },
        "misconceptions": [
          "重新配置不是凭空创造资源，而是改变同一资源的用途。",
          "市场投入多并不自动表示社会配置最优，未计价外部成本会造成过度配置。"
        ],
        "examApplication": [
          "明确资源从哪个用途转向哪个用途。",
          "用社会边际成本与收益解释过度或不足配置，而非只用产量大小。"
        ],
        "summary": [
          "配置决定生产要素的用途。",
          "激励变化会推动重新配置。",
          "市场价格遗漏外部影响时可能偏离社会最优。"
        ],
        "explanation": "资源配置把土地、劳动、资本和企业家才能安排到不同生产活动。市场价格和利润、政府预算与法规、家庭偏好都会影响流向。\n\n判断配置是否过度或不足，需要一个社会基准。若生产带来的第三方成本没有进入价格，某活动可能吸引过多资源；若教育等活动产生未获得补偿的外部收益，市场投入可能不足。"
      },
      "en": {
        "id": "u1-17-resource-allocation",
        "locale": "en",
        "title": "Resource allocation and reallocation",
        "takeaway": "Allocation directs factors of production; changes in prices, policy, or preferences cause reallocation and may address over- or underallocation.",
        "definition": "Resource allocation is the assignment of productive factors to different uses. Reallocation changes those uses, while overallocation and underallocation mean that too many or too few resources are devoted to an activity relative to the social optimum.",
        "causalChain": [
          "Alternative uses compete for scarce factors",
          "Prices, returns, and policy signal relative value",
          "Resources move toward uses with higher expected returns",
          "External costs or benefits can create over- or underallocation"
        ],
        "realWorldExample": {
          "title": "Carbon pricing encourages reallocation",
          "sourceUrl": "https://carbonpricingdashboard.worldbank.org/",
          "observedAt": "2026-09-29",
          "explanation": "The World Bank Carbon Pricing Dashboard records carbon taxes and emissions trading systems. Once emissions carry a cost, high-carbon activity becomes less attractive and capital or innovation may shift toward lower-carbon uses."
        },
        "misconceptions": [
          "Reallocation does not create resources; it changes how the same resources are used.",
          "A large market allocation is not necessarily socially optimal when prices omit external costs."
        ],
        "examApplication": [
          "State both the use resources leave and the use they enter.",
          "Explain over- or underallocation through social marginal costs and benefits, not output size alone."
        ],
        "summary": [
          "Allocation determines the uses of productive factors.",
          "Changing incentives cause reallocation.",
          "Omitted external effects can separate market and social outcomes."
        ],
        "explanation": "Resource allocation places land, labour, capital, and entrepreneurship in different productive activities. Market prices and profit, government budgets and rules, and household preferences can all direct those flows.\n\nOver- or underallocation requires a social benchmark. An activity may attract excessive resources if third-party costs are absent from its price. Education and similar activities may receive too little market investment when some benefits accrue beyond the buyer."
      }
    },
